From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ncsi: bump Mellanox MT_ 14.32 single-port GMA MAC to base+2

The Mellanox OEM Get-MAC (GMA) response semantics differ by firmware. The
MT_ ConnectX-4 firmware (14.32.x) returns base_mac+1 on a single-port card,
one short of the dedicated BMC management MAC (base+2) printed on the NIC
label and expected by the host BIOS / OEM firmware. The FB_ firmware
(e.g. 14.20.x) already returns the correct base+2, so an unconditional
single-port +1 over-shoots it to base+3.

Both variants enumerate channel_num == 1 and the GMA carries a single MAC,
so they cannot be told apart from the GMA response; gate the +1 on the
per-channel firmware version captured by the GVI handler. fw_version is
encoded [major:8][minor:8][subminor:16]; 14.32 is 0x0E20 in the top 16
bits. Only that firmware gets the correction; everything else (FB_ cards,
dual-port, non-Mellanox) trusts the GMA unchanged.

Signed-off-by: Flax <hmccanne@exclamationpoint.com>
---
--- a/net/ncsi/ncsi-rsp.c
+++ b/net/ncsi/ncsi-rsp.c
@@ -649,6 +649,27 @@
 	memcpy(saddr.sa_data, &rsp->data[mac_addr_off], ETH_ALEN);
 	if (mfr_id == NCSI_OEM_MFR_BCM_ID || mfr_id == NCSI_OEM_MFR_INTEL_ID)
 		eth_addr_inc((u8 *)saddr.sa_data);
+
+	/*
+	 * Mellanox OEM Get-MAC returns base_mac + (number of host ports): on a
+	 * dual-port ConnectX-4 OCP card that lands on the dedicated BMC
+	 * management MAC (base+2), but on a single-port card it can be one short
+	 * (base+1). These "Host Management" cards reserve base+2 as the BMC MAC,
+	 * so a single-port card may need a +1 bump. The bump is NOT universal,
+	 * though: it is a quirk of the MT_ ConnectX-4 firmware (14.32.x), which
+	 * returns base+1. The FB_ firmware (e.g. 14.20.x) already returns the
+	 * correct base+2 management MAC, so bumping it would over-shoot to
+	 * base+3. Both variants report channel_num == 1 and the GMA response
+	 * carries a single MAC, so the only way to tell them apart is the
+	 * firmware version from the GVI response (stored per channel). The
+	 * fw_version word is encoded [major:8][minor:8][subminor:16]; 14.32 is
+	 * 0x0E20 in the top 16 bits. Restrict the correction to that firmware.
+	 */
+	if (mfr_id == NCSI_OEM_MFR_MLX_ID && ndp->active_package &&
+	    ndp->active_package->channel_num == 1 && ndp->active_channel &&
+	    (ndp->active_channel->version.fw_version >> 16) == 0x0E20)
+		eth_addr_inc((u8 *)saddr.sa_data);
+
 	if (!is_valid_ether_addr((const u8 *)saddr.sa_data))
 		return -ENXIO;
 
